  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was Monday post-Halloween\u2014a structural headwind that depressed the day by design. Port Washington held up well despite it: core grooming revenue actually grew 8% week-over-week to $2,965, and your trailing-30d productivity per groomer sits at $482, only 2% below baseline. The real problem is Hicksville, which is underperforming at scale. Yesterday it produced only $250 per groomer against a $408 baseline\u2014a 39% productivity collapse. Capacity is the culprit: Hicksville ran 2 groomer-days yesterday versus its prior-month average of 4.5 per day. MTD, you're at 1.5 groomer-days per day there versus 6.0 in Port Washington. This is structural understaffing, not demand weakness.\n\nOn the flip side, Glen Cove delivered a strong signal yesterday\u2014Julianne D produced $405 on a single groomer, 72% above the 30-day baseline. The store is performing ahead of expectation on limited capacity. However, Glen Cove has zero groomer-days scheduled for today and tomorrow, which will kill momentum.\n\nOne bright spot: Port Washington's add-on attachment fell to 38% from 46%, and retail cratered 48% to $290. Flag the team on upsell discipline there\u2014this isn't volume-driven; it's execution. Hicksville's add-on revenue similarly missed. Across both core locations, your lapse pool represents $510k in annual value; prioritize reactivation outreach this week. Inventory reorders are non-critical but flagged.\n\n**Today you have 6 groomer-days scheduled at Port Washington and 3 at Hicksville (9 total); tomorrow you scale to 7 at Port Washington and 4 at Hicksville (11 total) with 15 bather hours; next 7 days: 58 groomer-days scheduled across all locations with 83 bather hours.**"
